\n\n1. **Every Monday at 03:00** runs `/usr/bin/backup`. It's a weekly backup, so if it fails silently you could go up to a week without a good one.\n2. **Every 15 minutes, all day, every day** (at :00, :15, :30 and :45) runs `/usr/bin/sync`. That's 96 runs a day. If a run takes longer than 15 minutes, runs will overlap unless the script guards against it.\n\n- **Timezone:** cron uses the host's timezone, so \"03:00\" means 03:00 on whichever machine runs this.\n- **Output and alerts:** neither line redirects output or sends alerts. Cron will try to mail any output to the crontab's owner, which is probably your ex-teammate. Check where that mail goes now, or you may miss failures.\n- **What the commands do:** I only read the crontab. I haven't looked at what `/usr/bin/backup` and `/usr/bin/sync` actually do, where they write, or what credentials they use. Those are the next things to check, along with which host and user the crontab is installed under.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}